BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Creating Classroom Libraries: Centering Representation in Children
 ’s Liter…
DTSTART;TZID=America/Chicago:20261001T090000
DTEND;TZID=America/Chicago:20261001T150000
DTSTAMP:20260920T125435Z
UID:e31c1e0195ebdd6f5efd93853f8e09a7@family-events-agent
SEQUENCE:0
CATEGORIES:museum,science,family,educators
DESCRIPTION:Organizer: Field Museum - Events\\nCost: Ticket required\\nAge
 s: All ages\\nCategory: General\\n\\nIllustration copyright (c) 2026 Krist
 en Balouch\; Published in 2026 by Red Comet Press\, LLC\, Brooklyn\, NY Jo
 in us at the Field Museum for an inspiring professional learning experienc
 e exploring how children's literature can help educators create affirming
 \, culturally responsive classroom libraries. Educators will examine how t
 houghtfully selected books can foster identity\, belonging\, empathy\, and
  critical thinking while expanding students' understanding of different co
 mmunities\, cultures\, and lived experiences. The workshop will feature a 
 keynote address by Dr. Taraneh Matloob\, Associate Professor of Literacy E
 ducation and children's literature scholar\, whose research and writing ce
 nter on multicultural children's literature and its role in literacy educa
 tion. Participants will explore practical strategies for evaluating and se
 lecting books that serve as both windows into others' experiences and mirr
 ors that reflect students' own identities. Drawing on Dr. Matloob's expert
 ise as both a scholar and picture book author\, educators will engage in d
 iscussion around representation\, perspective\, and the power of storytell
 ing in the elementary and middle grades. Participants will leave with tool
 s and resources to thoughtfully build and evaluate classroom libraries tha
 t reflect a range of cultures\, identities\, experiences\, and perspective
 s. As part of this unique experience\, attendees will enjoy a special view
 ing of items from the Field Museum's Iran Collection\, offering an opportu
 nity to connect literature with material culture and deepen understanding 
 of Persian history and storytelling traditions. The day will conclude with
  a meet-and-greet and book signing with Dr. Matloob for her picture books 
 An Invisible River to Cross and My Dear Friend . Whether you are building 
 a new classroom library or reexamining the books already on your shelves\,
  this workshop will provide practical strategies\, meaningful dialogue\, a
 nd museum-based resources to support thoughtful and culturally responsive 
 literacy instruction.\\n\\nMore info: https://www.fieldmuseum.org/our-even
 ts/professional-development-for-educators/creating-classroom-libraries-cen
 tering-representation-in-childrens-literature
LOCATION:Field Museum\, 1400 S Lake Shore Dr\, Chicago\, IL 60605
URL:https://www.fieldmuseum.org/our-events/professional-development-for-ed
 ucators/creating-classroom-libraries-centering-representation-in-childrens
 -literature
BEGIN:VALARM
ACTION:DISPLAY
